            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic appendectomy in a pediatric patient with type 1 Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>680-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2015.07.021</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(15)00226-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A pediatric patient with type 1 Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease-a disorder associated with a demyelinating polyneuropathy-presented for laparoscopic appendectomy in the setting of acute appendicitis. Induction and maintenance of anesthesia were successfully managed without the use of any depolarizing or nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocking agents. The patient was successfully extubated at the completion of the procedure without any respiratory or neuromuscular sequelae, with excellent pain control and no postoperative nausea or vomiting.</AbstractText>
